Introduction
------------

IBSimu is an ion optics simulation library with several capabilities
for solving electric fields in a geometry and tracing particles in the

electric fields. The code currently has following features:

1. Finite Difference Method (FDM) for solving Poisson's equation 1D, 2D 
   and 3D spaces and in the special case of cylindrical symmetry (3D 
   problem reduced to 2D).
2. Particle trajectory calculation in the solved potential.
3. Space charge density calculation from the trajectories .
4. Vlasov iteration using features 1 to 3.



Installing
----------

Ion Beam Simulator is a library written in C++ and is compilable by
modern gcc compilers in linux/unix environments. So far I haven't
tried to try to port the library into Microsoft Windows environment,
but I believe it shouldn't be a problem. You will need a valid C++
compiler to install and run the simulator. If the dependencies are
available and correctly installed, it should be pretty straightforward
to compile the library using GNU autotools build system:

1. First, run './configure' with necessary options.

   * One possible option is the '--prefix', which can be used to set
     the installation directory. For example '--prefix=/home/homedir/'
     to install under you home directory. Please note that GNU
     autotools does not support spaces in path names.

   * Other configuration option is the selection of conditional 
     compilation. The possible selectable compilations are:

     A. UMFPACKSolver class, selected with option '--with-umfpack'.
        You need to have UMFPACK and AMD installed with pkg-config
	packages to use this option.
     B. CSGObjectSolid class, selected with option '--with-csg'.
        You need to have CSG library installed for this option

2. Do 'make' to compile the library.

3. Do 'make check' to run tests distributed with the library.

4. Do 'make install' (as root if necessary).

If you install libraries in home directory, note that environment
variables need to be set. For example:

export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="/home/homedir/lib/pkgconfig"
export LD_LIBRARY_PATH="/home/homedir/lib"
export CXXFLAGS="-I/home/homedir/include"
export LDFLAGS="-L/home/homedir/lib"



Dependencies
------------

Ion Beam Simulator depends on other software to perform its functions.
IBSimu needs at least the following libraries:

- GSL -- GNU scientific library (http://www.gnu.org/software/gsl/).
- GTK+ -- GNOME Toolkit, library for graphical user interfaces 
  (http://www.gtk.org/).

These libraries are needed for optional features:

- CSG -- Constructive Solid Geometry library (available at 
  http://users.jyu.fi/~tvkalvas/code/csg/).
- SuiteSparse -- Sparse matrix package by Tim Davis (a modified copy 
  available at http://users.jyu.fi/~tvkalvas/code/suitesparse/).
- BLAS -- Basic linear algebra subprograms package (several versions
  available, for example the reference blas:
  http://www.netlib.org/blas/ (this is slow), or faster/better options:
  GOTO BLAS at http://www.tacc.utexas.edu/resources/software/ or 
  ATLAS at http://math-atlas.sourceforge.net/).



Compiling against the library
-----------------------------

The IBSimu library uses GNU autotools including pkg-config. Therefore
the compilation of a program is rather simple. For compilation with
GNU C++ compiler for example, do

> g++ -Wall -g `pkg-config --cflags ibsimu` -c -o simu.o simu.cpp

The linkin can be done by

> cc `pkg-config --libs ibsimu` simu.o -o simu



Documentation
-------------

IBSimu is using Doxygen documentation system for building the
reference manual from documentation blocks included in the source
code. See directory doc/html and for built HTML documentation or do
'make doc' for generating the docs.



